For a kidney stone, the treatment approach can vary depending on the size of the stone, its location, and whether it is causing a blockage or severe pain. Small stones might be able to pass on their own with adequate hydration and pain management, while larger stones or those causing obstruction may require more intervention, such as lithotripsy (a procedure that uses shock waves to break the stone into smaller pieces that can then pass out of the body) or surgical removal.

In both cases, the decision to admit you to the hospital for observation or to manage your condition as an outpatient would depend on the specifics of your diagnosis, the severity of your symptoms, and your overall health status. The healthcare team would discuss the best course of action with you, considering factors such as the risk of complications, the need for close monitoring, and the availability of appropriate care in an outpatient setting. The primary goal is always to ensure your safety and provide the most effective treatment for your condition.

